it is history in the making the second  impeachment trial of former president  trump begins in the senate he is charged  with inciting the capital assault that  resulted in the deaths of five people  including  a capitol police officer natalie brandt  has details in washington  in the same chamber that was stormed by  a mob of rioters on january 6  senators who were then eyewitnesses are  now jurors  in the historic second impeachment trial  of former president trump  accused of inciting the insurrection the  resolution is agreed to  first the senate approved a resolution  setting the rules then debate began over  whether the proceedings are  constitutional  they hated the results of the 2016  election  and want to use this impeachment process  to further their political agenda  mr trump's attorneys argue the senate  lacks the authority to try a president  who's no longer in office  while impeachment managers led by  constitutional scholar jamie raskin say  that argument has been rejected by legal  experts across the political spectrum  this cannot be the future of america we  cannot have  presidents inciting and mobilizing mob  violence against  our government a simple majority of  senators ultimately voted that the  proceedings are  constitutional setting the stage for the  rest of the trial  starting wednesday which is exactly five  weeks since the deadly capital riot  both sides will each have 16 hours over  two days to present their cases  senior aides to the house impeachment  manager said tuesday they plan to  present evidence nobody has seen before  president trump is singularly and  directly responsible  for inciting the assault on the capitol  we have  counter arguments to everything that  they raised  it's still unclear whether witnesses may  be called but the former president  is not expected to appear his attorneys  rejected that request calling it a  public relations stunt  as the trial continues inside the  capitol outside the national guard and  capitol police remain on high security  alert  natalie brand cbs news capitol hill
 

